\ save-system.4 - write the *running* Forth system out as a bootable
\ image, so work already compiled into the dictionary doesn't have to
\ be recompiled on every startup.
\
\ Why: `relfsh` compiles locals.4 + shell.4 from source every single
\ time it starts. Measured, 50 runs each: `relfsh -c true` 12.68s,
\ bare `relf kernel.img` 0.061s, `/bin/true` 0.040s. That is ~253ms
\ of compiling against ~1.2ms of actual engine startup - about 99.5%
\ of every invocation, and the reason the shell test suite takes a
\ minute. See PROGRESS.md's Iteration 40 entry.
\
\ This is deliberately NOT cross.4's SAVE-IMAGE. That one is a
\ host-side word that writes the target image the cross-compiler is
\ building. This one writes the image the current process is itself
\ running in, which is a different thing and much simpler, because
\ RelF images are already relocatable by design - that is the whole
\ point of RelF's relative addressing (see GOALS.md's load-bearing
\ architectural note).
\
\ Only three cells in a live system are absolute, and COLD is the
\ authority on which:
\
\   : COLD  START ! START @ FORTH-WORDLIST +! START @ DP +! ...
\
\ So exactly DP and FORTH-WORDLIST get START added at boot, and
\ subtracting it back out before writing is the entire "relocation"
\ this needs. BOOT (added to kernel.4 alongside this) is deliberately
\ kept as an offset at all times and never relocated in place, so it
\ needs no handling here at all.
\
\ Loadable against a plain kernel.img with no engine change - every
\ word used here already exists. Forces DECIMAL for its own literals
\ and restores the caller's BASE, for the reason documented in
\ locals.4's header.

BASE @  DECIMAL

VARIABLE SS-FID
VARIABLE SS-LEN

CREATE SS-MAGIC 8 ALLOT

: SS-SET-MAGIC ( --- )
  \ The 8-byte header relf.c checks at load: "RELF", then the cell
  \ width, then three zero bytes. A mismatched image is rejected
  \ cleanly at load rather than silently misbehaving, so this must
  \ agree with relf.c's own IMAGE_MAGIC exactly.
  [CHAR] R SS-MAGIC     C!
  [CHAR] E SS-MAGIC 1+  C!
  [CHAR] L SS-MAGIC 2 + C!
  [CHAR] F SS-MAGIC 3 + C!
  1 CELLS  SS-MAGIC 4 + C!
  0 SS-MAGIC 5 + C!
  0 SS-MAGIC 6 + C!
  0 SS-MAGIC 7 + C! ;

: SS-UNRELOCATE ( --- )   \ back to the on-disk, START-relative form
  START @ NEGATE DUP DP +! FORTH-WORDLIST +! ;

: SS-RERELOCATE ( --- )   \ and back to the live, absolute form
  START @ DUP DP +! FORTH-WORDLIST +! ;

: SAVE-SYSTEM ( c-addr u --- )
  \ Writes the whole live dictionary, START through HERE, prefixed by
  \ the magic header. The resulting file is a complete image: run it
  \ with `relf that-image` and every word defined at save time is
  \ already there.
  \
  \ HERE is read BEFORE unrelocating, because unrelocating changes DP
  \ (and therefore HERE) to a small offset. Likewise the file is
  \ created before unrelocating and closed after rerelocating, so the
  \ window in which the system is in its on-disk form contains
  \ nothing but the two writes - no ALLOT, no WORD, no HERE, and no
  \ ABORT" that could leave the system stranded in that state.
  HERE START @ - SS-LEN !
  SS-SET-MAGIC
  W/O BIN CREATE-FILE ABORT" SAVE-SYSTEM: cannot create file"
  SS-FID !
  SS-UNRELOCATE
  SS-MAGIC 8 SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  START @ SS-LEN @ SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  SS-RERELOCATE
  SS-FID @ CLOSE-FILE DROP ;

: SET-BOOT ( xt --- )
  \ Arrange for the saved image to run xt at startup instead of
  \ printing the banner and entering the interpreter loop. Stored as
  \ an offset from START, never as an absolute address, so it stays
  \ valid across the save and needs no relocation handling in
  \ SAVE-SYSTEM. COLD adds START back when it runs it - see kernel.4.
  START @ - BOOT ! ;

BASE !
